WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
"Drug Residues in Meat and
Their Effect on Antibiotic Resistance"

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.560
According to
the World Health Organization,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.640
more antibiotics
are fed to farm animals

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.040
to prevent disease
and fatten them faster

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.440
than are used
to treat sick people.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.800
This can foster the growth of
antibiotic-resistant superbugs

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.480
that can then get
transmitted to humans.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.640
As the UK's
Chief Medical Officer

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.200
once put it,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.280
"Every inappropriate
or unnecessary use

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.160
in animals or agriculture

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.880
is potentially signing
a death warrant

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.720
for a future patient.”

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.960
So, what are they:
miracle drugs or pig food?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.800
Here's how Dr. Greger
framed the issue

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.520
in a debate he had
with industry representatives,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.000
including from the
National Pork Producers Council.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.000
To ask if there's a question

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.960
between human drug resistance
and livestock antibiotic use

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.840
is like asking if there's
human-induced climate change,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.480
or if there's a link
between smoking and lung cancer.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.680
There are a few dissenting
industry scientists on one side,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:07.680
but then, basically,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.040
every other scientist
on the other.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.000
Both opposing camps
have scientists on their side,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.160
but only one really has
science on their side.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.120
One can just look at who's
on the sides of this debate.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.120
In one corner,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.560
you have
the World Health Organization,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:22.840
the American Medical
Association,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.680
the American Academy
of Pediatrics,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.000
American Public Health
Association,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.720
another 100

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.920
medical and public health
organizations in the country.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.800
On the other side,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.480
you have
the National Turkey Federation,

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.520
the National Chicken Council,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.600
the Sheep Industry Association,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.320
the National Pork Producers.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.520
It could not be more stark.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.360
Previously, Dr. Greger discussed

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.520
how feeding millions of pounds
of antibiotics

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.480
to cows, pigs, and chickens
every year is dangerous,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.520
because it fosters
antibiotic resistance--

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.600
not necessarily
because there's a problem

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.480
with antibiotic drug residues
getting into the meat itself.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.520
But, turns out,
he may be wrong.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.920
In 2010,
the U.S. Inspector General

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.920
slammed the U.S.
Department of Agriculture

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.880
for not protecting
the American public

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.320
from the contamination of meat

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.720
with residual drugs,
pesticides, and heavy metals.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.320
These drug and chemical residues

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.920
then find their way
to our dinner plates.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.200
But in order to safeguard
the nation's food supply

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.680
from harmful residues,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:25.680
the USDA,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.040
Food and Drug Administration,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.080
and Environmental
Protection Agency

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.680
are supposed to test
for these contaminants,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.200
and prevent adulterated meat
from entering the food supply.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.800
But, based on their review,
the U.S. Inspector General

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.520
found that
the National Residue Program

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.680
is not accomplishing its mission

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.160
of monitoring the food supply
for harmful residues.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.800
Regulators hadn't even
established threshold values

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.080
for many dangerous substances,

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.880
which has resulted
in contaminated meat

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.240
being distributed to consumers.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.840
What potential effects
could the drugs

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.200
and toxic metals in meat

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.240
have on people who consume it?

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.200
Gastrointestinal bleeding,
ulcers, allergic reactions,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.200
serious nerve damage,
severe inflammation,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.160
skin cancers,
internal cancers, jaundice,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.400
kidney failure,
neurotoxicity, and even death.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.480
Doesn't cooking
destroy the drugs, though?

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.480
No.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.200
No amount of cooking
will destroy residues.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.600
In fact, in some cases,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.120
heat may actually
break residues down

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.440
into components that are even
more harmful to consumers.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.280
The Pew Charitable Trusts
point out

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.600
that chemicals
that experts agree

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.600
pose a significant
public health hazard

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.320
are not routinely tested for,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.040
while others
that pose little risk

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.960
are routinely included
in sampling plans.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.480
The USDA does test
for some hazardous drugs though,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.080
and found that only about

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.760
1 in 260 samples
of animal products

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.520
had drug residue violations.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.720
But that suggests more than
100 million pounds of meat

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.280
adulterated with drugs

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.685
is served up every year
in the United States alone.

